// Expected: a1 at one session and at two, reported as a replay.
//
// Alice signs a fresh m and sends it to Bob, who verifies the signature and
// then seals m for whichever peer his scenario binds. The second scenario
// binds Bob's peer to Mallory, whose key is leaked, so that run of Bob is a
// corrupt-peer run and its clones of Alice are the same configuration as the
// honest one (the scenario rebinds only Bob). The attacker takes the flight
// Alice@2 sent to Bob@2 -- m@2 and SIGN(ska, m@2) -- and delivers it to the
// honest Bob, whose signature check passes. Nothing is forged: Alice signed
// m@2 herself, in the other run. Bob accepts a message Alice sent once and
// meant for a different run of Bob, so injective agreement fails, and that is
// a replay. It is not excused by the matching-run exemption, because the
// emitting run's recipient is the corrupt-peer configuration of Bob.
//
// The verdict was already a1. What this model pins is the classification: the
// sibling groups used to recognise a replay were built only over the session
// axis, so a scenario copy was never a sibling and the trace said m@2 was
// "sent by Attacker and not by Alice", one step after showing Alice signing
// it. The copy-axis groups are consulted only after the matching-run
// exemption has declined, so they can relabel an a1 as a replay but can never
// turn an exempted a0 into an attack; adding an identity scenario stays the
// no-op the `scenario` metamorphic property says it is.

attacker[active]

principal Alice[
	knows private ska
	pka = PUBKEY(ska)
	generates m
	sig = SIGN(ska, m)
]

principal Mallory[
	knows private msk
	mpk = PUBKEY(msk)
	leaks msk
]

principal Bob[
	knows public peer
]

Mallory -> Bob: [mpk]

Alice -> Bob: [pka], m, sig

principal Bob[
	_ = SIGNVERIF(pka, m, sig)?
	sealed = PKE_ENC(peer, m)
	_ = HASH(m)
]

scenarios[
	Bob[peer = pka]
	Bob[peer = mpk]
]

queries[
	authentication? Alice -> Bob: m
]
